7. Install IApplicationDbContext in Application inside Interfaces
8. We created Result.cs in Application -> Common -> Models
   so basically this method we can call with any method like Task<Result<Product>> CreateProduct(string name, int quantity)
   now here CreateProduct can have any input parameter that does not matter, it is basically the output type that is handled by result
   so we get Result<Product>.Success or Result<Product>.Failure 

// The INPUT parameters can be ANYTHING
Task<Result<Product>> CreateProduct(string name, int quantity)
Task<Result<Product>> CreateProduct(CreateProductRequest request)
Task<Result<Product>> CreateProduct(Guid id, string name, decimal price, int stock)

// What matters is the OUTPUT type: Result<Product>

Key Points:

1. Input = Flexible → Any parameters your method needs
2. Output = Standardized → Always Result<T> or Result
3. Caller knows → Must check .IsSuccess before using .Value

Input → [Method Logic] → Output

Any params     ↓         Result<T>.Success(data)
you need    Business      OR
           Logic       Result<T>.Failure(error)

9. We created IApplicationDbContext in Application -> Common -> Interfaces
Two Different Things:
Interface IApplicationDbContext → Application Layer (abstraction)
Implementation ApplicationDbContext → Infrastructure Layer (concrete class)

┌─────────────────────────────────┐
│    Application Layer            │  ← Defines WHAT it needs (interface)
│    - IApplicationDbContext      │     "I need a way to save products"
└─────────────────────────────────┘
            ↑ implements
            │
┌─────────────────────────────────┐
│    Infrastructure Layer         │  ← Provides HOW it's done (implementation)
│    - ApplicationDbContext       │     "Here's EF Core implementation"
│    - Uses EF Core               │
│    - Runs migrations            │
└─────────────────────────────────┘

// File: src/ECommerce.Application/Common/Interfaces/IApplicationDbContext.cs
namespace ECommerce.Application.Common.Interfaces;

public interface IApplicationDbContext  // ← Interface (contract)
{
    DbSet<Product> Products { get; }
    DbSet<Category> Categories { get; }
    
    Task<int> SaveChangesAsync(CancellationToken cancellationToken = default);
}

Says: "I need access to Products and Categories, and I need to save changes"
Doesn't care: How it's stored (SQL, MongoDB, in-memory), how migrations work

Infrastructure Layer (implements interface):

// File: src/ECommerce.Infrastructure/Persistence/ApplicationDbContext.cs
using ECommerce.Application.Common.Interfaces;  // ← Imports the interface
using Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore;

namespace ECommerce.Infrastructure.Persistence;

public class ApplicationDbContext : DbContext, IApplicationDbContext  // ← Implements interface
{
    public ApplicationDbContext(DbContextOptions<ApplicationDbContext> options) 
        : base(options)
    {
    }
    
    // Implement interface properties
    public DbSet<Product> Products => Set<Product>();
    public DbSet<Category> Categories => Set<Category>();
    public DbSet<ProductImage> ProductImages => Set<ProductImage>();
    
    // EF Core configuration
    protected override void OnModelCreating(ModelBuilder modelBuilder)
    {
        modelBuilder.ApplyConfigurationsFromAssembly(typeof(ApplicationDbContext).Assembly);
    }
    
    // Implement interface method
    public override async Task<int> SaveChangesAsync(CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        return await base.SaveChangesAsync(cancellationToken);
    }
}
Says: "Here's the concrete implementation using EF Core and PostgreSQL"
Benefits:

✅ Application doesn't know about EF Core
✅ Can test with mock/in-memory implementation
✅ Can swap databases without changing Application code
✅ Follows Dependency Inversion Principle

Application Layer
├── IApplicationDbContext.cs  ← Interface (contract)
│   └── "I need Products, Categories, SaveChanges"
│
│   Handlers inject interface ↓
│   
